it was recommended to me a couple of  weeks ago that I should do a video on  how to respond to about grading exam or  more broadly failure in academia and I  think this is a very important subject  and it is worth a video on its site and  even if it's just a quick one because a  lot of people will go for years in  academia in how a string of successes  and - no wrong whatsoever and all it  takes is one bad grade or one bad set of  exams and it can be curtains it can  finish off their academic careers no one  wants that fragility to their academic  career so even if you've never had a  serious set of academic failures I think  it's worth watching this video now some  people might look at my channel and say  well you went to Oxford you clearly did  very well in all your exams what on  earth can you say about failure academic  baby well I've had my fair share of  academic failure and I'm going to talk a  little bit about that later in the video  my point is that I have achieved the  things like achieve in academia  not necessarily despite failures but in  a way because of them because my opinion  is that you can view a bad grade a bad  series of exams in one of two ways and  this actually doesn't just apply to  academia this applies to anything in  life like my breakup or not doing well  in a sports match you can view these bad  events in one of two ways and this is  informed incidentally by reading most  notably a book called mindset by Carol  Dweck you can view bad events in one of  two ways  either you can view something bad  happening like a bad grade as being  purely reflective on your worth as a  person if I get a bad grade in an exam  that indicates to me that I am not smart  enough to do well that I am not as  clever as those people who got A's and a  stars in a more broader sense if this  was sports or music it would indicate  that I am not as good at football or  rugby or playing the clarinet or the  piano as the person who do better than  me fundamentally I am NOT  as good at X than this person was if you  choose to view bad events in this way  then you can kind of see why people go  off the rails from that point onwards  because you might say to yourself well  if I'm not good enough to get a good  grade why am I even bothering you know  I'm clearly just stupid I'm not good  enough I'm not going to try and often  people who have this mindset will do  exactly that in tests they have been  shown to once they've been given a  difficult problem and have received a  bad mark whatever it may be in that  problem that point onwards their level  of effort decreases their attainment  correspondingly decreases the second way  of viewing a bad mark is as follows it  doesn't represent who you are as a  person it doesn't tell you anything  about you fundamentally what it is  instead is an opportunity to improve a  difficult math problem shouldn't  necessarily be oh I can't do this I'm  not good enough I'm just stupid instead  it should be this is an opportunity for  me to improve if I can do this problem  that's going to make me smarter equally  if you were to say fail music exam it  will tell you not I'm just not good at  playing the piano instead it should tell  you that if I can pass this exam it's an  opportunity to improve it's an  opportunity to make myself better at  playing the piano and so it is with a  bad grade if you receive a C or a D in a  subject that shouldn't say to you I'm  just not good at it it should say T this  is an opportunity to improve I can make  myself better at this subject and again  in tests people who have this mindset of  bad effects improve over time and it's  actually surprising to have documented  it all it takes is a mindset of  improvement it takes the ability to look  at a bad situation and go you know what  this doesn't mean anything like me as a  person what it is is a chance to improve  it's an opportunity to improve myself to  return to a point I made earlier about  me people might not think that I know  nothing of academic failure  well something which I've never  mentioned before is that I failed my  first year Oxford  she point out I wasn't alone in doing  this it was quite a widely failed set of  exams by a lot of people but all the  same I didn't exactly do well I'm really  looking back on it I had two options I  could either view that failure and and  time at the fact that I came into Oxford  thinking I don't think I'm as good as  these other people and I'm assuming them  at the very bottom of the Year at time  out in with failing the year I could  have very easily just thought you know  what I'm not good enough for this I'm  just going to drop out I can't do this  but although it didn't consciously  realize at the time what I thought was  no you know what I'm going to work  harder I'm going to improve myself I'm  going to show these people that I can  take these exams that I can do physics  so much better than I could before and I  did I took my resuts and almost got a  first on average I was a half a percent  off a first on average it's not  necessarily the easiest thing to do but  you have to sometimes look at failure in  the face and say I'm not going to let  this define me I am going to learn from  this I'm going to use this to improve  myself if you keep with that mindset and  if you keep trying keep applying effort  in the right way failure won't define  you success will please comment if you  found this video useful by the way and  also if you have any stories of your own  of times when you've overcome academic  setbacks and come back stronger from the  experience or in sport or music or  really anything in life please leave  those in the comments below cuz I'd be  really interested to see how my  subscribers have come to terms with not  necessarily doing as well as they might  like and also share your story inspire  others

